WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:03.900
My next guest is also in this book.

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.166
This is gonna be the thread
through the whole thing, okay.

00:00:06.166 --> 00:00:07.830
I thought you were reading this book.

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.493
Please welcome Jay Larson.

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:11.420
Jay how are you?

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:12.520
[Jay] I'm great.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:15.340
I didn't know if Austin was
done dropping any more credits,

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:16.270
you know what I mean?

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:18.367
He's gonna pop up as a picture in picture,

00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:21.890
like when they play, you
know, the less important game,

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:22.723
that's a blowout

00:00:22.723 --> 00:00:24.950
and he's gonna be just
dropping credits in there.

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:26.560
'Cause he's booking things as we speak.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:27.910
He just booked right now.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:28.743
Are you working?

00:00:28.743 --> 00:00:30.700
Are you staying busy?
Oh yeah look at this.

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.470
[Jay] I saw you asked Austin
if he's done any commercials,

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:37.760
I directed a commercial
during this whole thing.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:39.310
Yeah, and then just for future reference,

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:40.143
if you could take all

00:00:40.143 --> 00:00:43.010
of your questions based
off of Austin credits,

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:45.929
it's gonna help us do a
great thread for the show.

00:00:45.929 --> 00:00:47.100
[Jay] That's you know what I mean?

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.700
I like unity and concise.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:50.740
Yeah, okay.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:53.670
So you directed a
commercial pandemic wise,

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:56.360
which is it's already difficult
enough to do a commercial

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:57.740
cause it's a hodgepodge crew.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.310
People that have not worked together.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:00.143
[Jay] Yeah.

00:01:00.143 --> 00:01:01.610
Are we, are you doing this remotely

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:02.490
Or are you onset?

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:04.020
No, we did on set, we did two days.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:05.893
One day on set in a church

00:01:05.893 --> 00:01:08.440
and then another day out at a golf course.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:09.741
Are you getting rapid tested before

00:01:09.741 --> 00:01:11.020
or are they just winging it?

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:12.981
Got tested day before
we had someone on set.

00:01:12.981 --> 00:01:16.979
You got your temp checked
three times throughout the day.

00:01:16.979 --> 00:01:19.180
Everyone's in masks, social distance,

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:20.370
masks off for shooting.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:22.250
But you're in a mask the whole time?

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:23.083
Yeah.

00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:24.780
How does that work, cause you like to,

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.260
when you direct you like
to scream an actor's faces.

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.723
[Jay] I get in people's
faces big time, bro.

00:01:32.516 --> 00:01:35.540
You have a breakfast so
you're, you're on your game.

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:37.102
Don't eat my raisins.

00:01:37.102 --> 00:01:39.130
[Jay] Get away from craft, sir.

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:40.890
Kevin Nealan was our lead.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:44.380
And so like, I have such
respect for Kevin Nealon,

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.470
like trying to give
direction, I'd be like,

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:47.780
Hey Kevin, what if we, and he's like,

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:49.440
Oh yeah, you're gonna love this.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:50.680
You're going to love what I do here.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.180
And then everyone would just be laughing,

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:54.475
you're like, yeah, yeah,
let's let's move on.

00:01:54.475 --> 00:01:55.950
Don't got to do anything.

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:56.783
Yeah it was great.

00:01:56.783 --> 00:01:58.770
Are you going to do more of
those or was it such a weird

00:01:58.770 --> 00:01:59.612
experience or was just like,

00:01:59.612 --> 00:02:02.690
let's just wait or you like work?

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:03.940
[Jay] I had no problem with it.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.120
I'm on a, I write on a show

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:09.160
and I wrote and directed five sketches for

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.492
the show during the pandemic as well.

00:02:11.492 --> 00:02:14.040
I think if everyone is just
cautious and you're smart

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:15.380
and you're safe.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:17.460
I mean, you're seeing
commercials with people in masks,

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.670
so clearly they're making
content or shooting commercials

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.209
during Covid, you know what I mean?

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:26.690
Unless Jeep grand Cherokee
knew about this and they

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:28.023
released the virus.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:30.533
Are they behind it?

00:02:30.533 --> 00:02:33.470
Is this something Lee Iacocca, you know,

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:34.610
was ahead of the game on,

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:36.730
I know that might be an old
school reference for you and

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:37.940
maybe some of the audience,

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:42.290
but I'm educated and I'm informed,

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:43.290
you know what I mean?

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.610
Yeah, you're informed.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:49.710
It is, it does change the
relationship onset a little bit.

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:51.800
It's a little less collaborative
and that everyone's afraid

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.250
of each other.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.960
And also the writing room can be weird.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:59.635
I've been in some writing rooms
now where you don't know if

00:02:59.635 --> 00:03:00.882
your joke that pitch bombed

00:03:00.882 --> 00:03:03.034
or it's zoom bombed and it just crashed.

00:03:03.034 --> 00:03:05.200
[Jay] Yeah, or there was just a delay.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:06.690
What if it, it crushed?

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:10.160
But the delay was so long,
you missed the tire response.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:11.570
Yeah, and then you already back out,

00:03:11.570 --> 00:03:13.400
like if someone pitches a
joke, it's like, oh yeah,

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.550
so then he fills up the back
of the truck with a bunch of

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:17.573
toasters and then that falls.

00:03:17.573 --> 00:03:20.110
But yeah, I don't think we
have the budget for that.

00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:21.900
So we'll just, yeah, we'll move on.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.070
[Jay] Yeah, well we can, we
can rip on the guys maybe,

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:25.370
maybe later.

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:26.450
Yeah, I'm in a writer's room,

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:29.550
I have been the entire
pandemic and it's like crazy.

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:31.970
We have some people in
Florida and some people in LA.

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:33.540
So we were, you know,

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:36.450
like working out times
and just making it work.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.300
You just get so comfortable on zoom,

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:39.133
just being like, well,

00:03:39.133 --> 00:03:40.303
let's just make it work.

00:03:40.303 --> 00:03:42.280
I think moving forward,

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:44.640
industry-wise writer's room and even

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.609
some sets will be a little
bit more remote because we've

00:03:47.609 --> 00:03:49.230
proved that it can be done.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:51.770
[Jay] I hope not. I hope that listen,

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:54.037
I'm all about being safe and
we've been like completely safe

00:03:54.037 --> 00:03:56.250
with our kids and like groceries.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:57.820
I haven't eaten out anywhere,

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.637
but I also believe that
like when we get control-

00:04:00.637 --> 00:04:02.365
I say DJ Khalid, yeah.

00:04:02.365 --> 00:04:03.198
You know what I mean?

00:04:03.198 --> 00:04:04.150
Once we get control of this,

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:05.640
that we should,

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.930
writer's room you need
to be next to people,

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:10.066
with people, riffing,

00:04:10.066 --> 00:04:12.936
like getting a sense for them
seeing them, like that's like,

00:04:12.936 --> 00:04:13.897
there's such a,

00:04:13.897 --> 00:04:17.165
it's such a part of the creative
process that I think like,

00:04:17.165 --> 00:04:20.044
I hope maybe there will
be some rooms that will,

00:04:20.044 --> 00:04:23.220
I think we should use it as
like an advantage to be able to

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:24.660
be remote if someone needs to be.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:27.300
But otherwise I think
being in a room together,

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.780
like really benefits the writing.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:31.270
Yeah, absolutely.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:33.900
Because it is an energy thing
where you need to throw out a

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:36.540
bunch of bad ideas to get to the good one.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:38.040
And you can only really feel safe

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:39.627
doing that when everyone's doing that,

00:04:39.627 --> 00:04:41.645
and you're all in the same
room and you can pick up on

00:04:41.645 --> 00:04:43.680
certain social cues,

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:44.820
like you'll read comments with this,

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:46.910
I could live habits like,
Oh, this podcast boring.

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:47.743
And it's like, well,

00:04:47.743 --> 00:04:50.140
it's just different than
being on stage having to

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:51.460
deal with this delay.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:53.266
I also think when you're in a zoom room,

00:04:53.266 --> 00:04:56.080
you're constantly like the
clock is constantly ticking.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:57.180
Like when is this ending?

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:58.530
Whereas when you're in the writer's room,

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:01.240
so you might just like accept
joke because you're like,

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:02.620
well, this thing has
got to end some point.

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:03.940
Let's just get that joke done.

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:05.253
Whereas in a writer's room
you could like be like,

00:05:05.253 --> 00:05:06.970
all right, let's just wait on that.

00:05:06.970 --> 00:05:09.960
Let's go work on this. And
like, oh dude! You know?

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:11.380
How are you handling it with kids?

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:13.060
Are you homeschooling?

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:15.010
You're supervising homeschooling.

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:15.960
I'm not a big,

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:20.160
I don't want my six and a half
year old on three hours of

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:21.070
zoom call.

00:05:21.070 --> 00:05:22.920
So we de-enrolled them from school

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:24.860
and set up our home as a homeschool,

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:26.880
and then we hired a teacher,

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:28.840
that we like went through
the COVID process with.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:32.170
So now she like every day
nine to two is with my kids,

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:35.190
and like they do project
based learning activities,

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:36.023
learning.

00:05:36.023 --> 00:05:37.470
They do field trips, you know,

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:41.370
it's a lot more organic and
a higher engagement level

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:43.160
than zoom.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.460
So you hired, so your
essentially a principal.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:48.050
Yeah, I'm VP, my wife got the principal.

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:49.140
I mean, it's not a big deal.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.000
It's not a huge deal.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:52.540
Seem really upset about it.

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:53.870
[Jay] It hasn't been a thing, dude.

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:56.510
It hasn't come up. It
hasn't been an issue.

00:05:56.510 --> 00:05:58.752
I'm not saying I have better
credentials or whatever,

00:05:58.752 --> 00:06:00.032
You know what I mean?

00:06:00.032 --> 00:06:02.000
Do you have the same power as your,

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.330
your wife as the principal.

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:05.290
[Jay] Don't, you know, the
structure of the principal,

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:06.290
vice principal?

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:07.470
I'm the fun guy.

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:10.130
Vice-principal kind of
seems like, lunch is over,

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:11.420
you know, kind of serve at the lunch room

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:12.360
and then you're back in.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:13.340
[Jay] Yeah, that sounds right.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:15.660
That's kind of, that's pretty accurate.

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:18.414
You move everyone's cars when
the car needs to back out.

00:06:18.414 --> 00:06:20.910
[Jay] You gotta move cars, you know.

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:22.870
Hopefully you'll get a promotion,

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:26.750
So it's also on you to
kind of keep the kids busy.

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:27.860
I saw you're building stuff,

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:32.860
You built a whole science
experiment table outside.

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:34.980
I just love being
interactive with my kids,

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:36.730
and so we built a science table.

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:38.620
I built them a Lego table.

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:40.730
I like doing adventures with them.

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:42.400
Like when the whole thing started,

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:44.850
my job was like the
first two weeks of COVID,

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:46.730
they were like, let's just be loose.

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:47.810
And the kids were out of school,

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:49.300
so like, I would take them on hikes

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:50.610
and then find a picnic table,

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:53.570
and we'd like collect leaves
and turn them into projects.

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:57.670
I just liked that idea, of
like constantly making learning

00:06:57.670 --> 00:06:59.930
a fun experience, you know?

00:06:59.930 --> 00:07:01.770
Yeah, it's a great time
to be picking up leaves

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:04.070
and anything you find
on the street right now.

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:08.510
You know, like, what is that bacteria?

00:07:08.510 --> 00:07:10.720
What better way to learn about COVID?

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:11.853
Getting COVID.

00:07:11.853 --> 00:07:14.240
I think your kids are
gonna find the vaccine,

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:15.800
on one of your leaf trips,

00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:17.980
which is actually them
just cleaning the yard.

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:19.650
Listen, when you say leaf trip,

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:22.900
it almost sounds like
you're de-valuing it.

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:24.810
No, it sounds like I
actually want to go on it.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:27.050
It sounds like you're a
great dad and talking to you,

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:29.257
makes me wish I had a great dad,

00:07:29.257 --> 00:07:31.600
because it sounds, it sounds fun.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:32.510
It is fun. Yeah.

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:33.750
It is very fun. They're fun.

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:36.850
They're wild kids. So they,
they, they take to it.

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:38.250
What else have you been
doing to keep them busy?

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:39.940
You've been showing them movies?

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:40.773
No.

00:07:40.773 --> 00:07:41.620
Not at all.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:45.420
[Jay] You know, what I did
is I've been doing, like,

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:47.130
we did this field trip to the beach.

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:49.179
We went to the beach with
like my buddy and his kids,

00:07:49.179 --> 00:07:51.010
but we've been watching Goonies.

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:52.201
Are you a Goonies fan?

00:07:52.201 --> 00:07:55.400
I wasn't allowed to watch
a lot of movies growing up.

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.200
Like I thought Star Wars
was the most boring movie,

00:07:57.200 --> 00:08:00.050
Cause my parents had edited
all the violent parts out.

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:01.110
So it's just a movie,

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:01.970
a bunch of people talking

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:03.979
and then a brown bathrobe
falls on the ground.

00:08:03.979 --> 00:08:05.736
Totally sucks.

00:08:05.736 --> 00:08:07.900
No, I have, I've seen clips of it,

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:10.660
but I haven't seen the
entirety of the movie.

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:13.680
[Jay] Yeah, well Goonies,
Goonies is, you know,

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:14.650
it's Spielberg dude.

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:16.820
You're like, dude, this
is Steven Spielberg.

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:19.430
And we showed it to the kids
and they fell in love with it.

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:20.263
And you know,

00:08:20.263 --> 00:08:22.650
there's a treasure hunt and
there's a map they follow.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:25.940
So after we watch it, I took
the kids to El Matador beach,

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:27.980
which has got like rocks and caves.

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:31.370
And I made the night before, a
treasure map and made it look

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:32.870
old and like, you know,

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:36.130
put old writing on it
and put like the rocks.

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:38.390
I drew the rocks and put X marks the spots

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:39.360
so we got to the beach,

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:42.380
buried it in the sand by our mat.

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:43.560
And then when my buddy got there,

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:47.029
I went and took like a stick
that was charred and put Xs on

00:08:47.029 --> 00:08:50.260
the rocks where I had
written them on the map.

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:51.550
And then at lunch, I was just like,

00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:54.220
have my hand in the sand and
I was like, Oh, what's this?

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:56.868
And I hand it to my son
and he was just like,

00:08:56.868 --> 00:08:58.170
(gasping) Dad!

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:01.990
And like the kids lost their minds.

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:03.210
And then we went on the treasure hunt.

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:04.510
We found the Xs,

00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:08.720
I bottled coins or brought
them and then hid them

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:09.943
and the kids found them.

00:09:10.807 --> 00:09:12.890
And then it just turned into like,

00:09:12.890 --> 00:09:14.190
their whole world was like,

00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:15.840
we got to find more treasure dad,

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:16.690
we've got to find it.

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:17.830
We got to dig.

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:19.980
And I was like, I looked at my buddy.

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:22.260
I'm like, should we tell them
like that this isn't real?

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:24.050
And he's like, no, dude, let them go.

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:25.140
I'm like, all right.

00:09:25.140 --> 00:09:26.190
So now they just like,

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:29.863
they believe that One-Eyed
Willy from Goonies lived.

00:09:29.863 --> 00:09:32.840
So now they're just going to
